# Vault AppRole Authentication and MLflow Startup

## 1. Task Overview

The objective of this task was to correct Vault's `mlflow-reader` policy, enable AppRole authentication, and create an AppRole named `mlflow` bound to that policy. These changes allow the MLflow wrapper to authenticate with Vault and access the required KV v2 secret.

The final requirement was to confirm that the MLflow tracking server responds successfully on port `5000`.

## 2. Environment

| Component | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Vault address | `http://127.0.0.1:8200` |
| Vault UI port | `8200` |
| MLflow port | `5000` |
| Policy name | `mlflow-reader` |
| KV v2 API path | `secret/data/mlflow` |
| Authentication method | AppRole |
| AppRole name | `mlflow` |
| Policy capability | `read` |

## 3. Configure Vault CLI Access

The Vault CLI was configured to use the lab's HTTP endpoint and the existing root token file.

```bash
export VAULT_ADDR="http://127.0.0.1:8200"
export VAULT_TOKEN="$(cat /root/code/vault-root-token)"
```

This resolved the initial HTTPS-versus-HTTP connection error.

## 4. Correct the `mlflow-reader` Policy

The existing policy was updated to grant read access to the KV v2 API data path `secret/data/mlflow`.

```bash
vault policy write mlflow-reader - <<'EOF'
path "secret/data/mlflow" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
EOF
```

### Preview: Actual Terminal Output

```text
Success! Uploaded policy: mlflow-reader
```

The policy name was preserved, and the policy was updated successfully.

### Verify the Policy

```bash
vault policy read mlflow-reader
```

Expected policy content:

```hcl
path "secret/data/mlflow" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
```

## 5. Enable AppRole Authentication

AppRole was enabled so that the MLflow wrapper could authenticate as a machine identity instead of relying on a static root token.

```bash
vault auth enable approle
```

### Preview: Actual Terminal Output

```text
Success! Enabled approle auth method at: approle/
```

## 6. Create the MLflow AppRole

The `mlflow` role was created and associated with the `mlflow-reader` policy.

```bash
vault write auth/approle/role/mlflow \
  token_policies="mlflow-reader" \
  token_ttl="1h" \
  token_max_ttl="4h"
```

### Preview: Actual Terminal Output

```text
Success! Data written to: auth/approle/role/mlflow
```

### Verify the Role

```bash
vault read auth/approle/role/mlflow
```

